docs {
  Confirmed live 2026-09-07 against "David's Dev Account", against a
  throwaway category created and destroyed in the same test run (id
  `6a9f1d2e2b6fd055ffb7c804`, no longer exists -- this file's `http` block
  uses a placeholder since there's nothing live to point it at).

  ## Major finding -- resolves `USER_FLOWS.md`'s central open question
  `openapi_pimv3.json` declares **no** `PATCH`/`DELETE` for
  `/product-categories/{identifier}` at all. Both work anyway:

  - `PATCH /product-categories/{id}` with `{"name": "..."}` -- **200**,
    renames the category (confirmed via a follow-up `GET` showing the new
    name). Same response shape as `POST`'s create-output (partial, missing
    `account_id`/`created`).
  - `DELETE /product-categories/{id}` -- **204 No Content**, and a
    follow-up `GET` on the same id then returns **404**
    (`{"errors":[{"name":"NotFoundError","description":"Document with id
    ... not found"}]}`) -- genuinely deleted, not a soft-delete.
  - `GET /product-categories/{id}/{path}` on a deleted id -- **404**
    `{"errors":[{"name":"NotFoundError","description":"Resource not
    found"}]}` (a different, simpler error shape than the "field not
    found" 400 used for an invalid `{path}` on a *live* resource).

  This same undeclared PATCH/DELETE pattern was spot-checked and confirmed
  working across every other Phase 1 resource this session (asset
  categories, attribute groups, product attributes, connections, import
  profiles -- see each folder's own README). Strongly suggests the pattern
  generalizes to every v3 resource, including ones later phases will test
  (Pim Product Lists, Asset Lists, Channels, Ecatalogs, etc.) -- confirm
  per-resource rather than assuming, but this is no longer an open
  question in the "does undeclared PATCH/DELETE exist at all" sense that
  `USER_FLOWS.md` raised it as.
}
